=== capability step 런북을 executor가 실제로 소비 (2026-07-26, in-process, $0) ===

WHAT WAS WRONG
  `capability_schema` could always express ordering, `condition`, `on_failure`
  and a per-step `verify` naming how to prove the step helped. Nothing consumed
  any of it:

    - `DecisionOutput` carried only a flat `actions: list[str]`.
    - `_resolve_runbook_actions` resolved the flat `capabilities` list; `steps`
      was never read.
    - `CAPABILITY_RUNBOOKS` — 9 runbooks with full step definitions — was
      referenced by NOTHING outside its own tests. All five selectable runbook
      ids also live there, so every one of those step definitions was dead data.

  The tests were green throughout, because they tested the declaration.

--- fix 1: steps reach the executor ---
  DecisionOutput.steps (default [] = the old behaviour, exactly)
  _resolve_runbook_steps() resolves each declared step's capability -> action,
  keeping order, condition, on_failure and verify.
  `actions` is derived from the steps in step order, so the Slack report, the
  incident record and every existing consumer see an unchanged shape.

--- fix 2: the built-in catalog was unreachable ---
  The runbook row wins (an operator editing DynamoDB must be able to change the
  plan); CAPABILITY_RUNBOOKS fills in when the row declares no steps. That one
  lookup makes nine runbooks' worth of ordering and verification live.

--- fix 3: THE ONE THE UNIT TESTS COULD NOT CATCH ---
First real pipeline run, log-only, alert = pod OOMKilled:

  decision.runbook ... step_count=2
  steps:
    - restart_pod  cap=restart_workload  action=ONPREM-RolloutRestartWorkload
      condition=None  on_failure=continue  verify=assert_workload_ready
    - scale_nodes  cap=scale_out         action=ONPREM-ScaleWorkload
      condition={'previous_step_failed': True}  on_failure=abort
      verify=assert_replica_count

  executed : ['ONPREM-RolloutRestartWorkload', 'ONPREM-ScaleWorkload']   <-- WRONG
  skipped  : []

  `scale_nodes` is conditioned on the restart having FAILED. The restart
  succeeded. It should have been skipped, and it ran.

  Cause: `_deserialise_decision` did not carry `steps`. The pipeline serialises
  the decision between stages, so the executor received a DecisionOutput with an
  empty step list and silently fell back to the flat path — conditions,
  on_failure and verify all discarded, nothing in the logs to say so.

  Every unit test passed because each built DecisionOutput in memory and never
  crossed that boundary. Declaring a thing and carrying it across a boundary are
  different problems, and only the second one is what runs in production.

  After carrying `steps` through:
    executor.step.condition_false  step=scale_nodes action=ONPREM-ScaleWorkload
    executed : ['ONPREM-RolloutRestartWorkload']
    skipped  : ['ONPREM-ScaleWorkload']
    resolved : False            <-- still wrong, differently

--- fix 4: a condition-skip is not a failure ---
  With conditions finally honoured, `resolution_verdict` counted the skip
  against resolution — so the runbook's SUCCESS path (restart worked, therefore
  do not scale) reported `resolved=False`.

  That is the same inversion log-only mode already taught us: "we correctly
  chose not to act" became "we tried and could not". It would make every
  properly-conditioned runbook look broken precisely when it worked, and the
  better the runbook, the worse it would look.

  `resolution_verdict(..., not_applicable=[...])` now exempts condition-skips.
  They are still reported as skipped (transparency); they just do not withhold
  resolution. A step that TRIED and failed still does.

--- final, same alert ---
  decision.runbook ... step_count=2
  executor.step.condition_false  step=scale_nodes
  executed : ['ONPREM-RolloutRestartWorkload']
  skipped  : ['ONPREM-ScaleWorkload']
  resolved : True

  Before this work the same incident produced
  executed=[restart, scale] / skipped=[] — it scaled out a node group that did
  not need scaling, because the condition saying otherwise was never read.

--- scope ---
  The On-Prem in-process pipeline calls the same decision/executor handlers as
  the AWS Step Functions path, so both are covered by one change.

  NOT done: four runbooks in CAPABILITY_RUNBOOKS (certificate-expiry, disk-full,
  health-check-failure, network-latency-high) are still unreachable — they have
  no entry in BUILTIN_RUNBOOKS, so nothing can select them. Separate gap, left
  alone rather than quietly widened.

  Guards: tests/test_executor_capability_steps.py (order, conditions,
  on_failure, declared-verify precedence, resolution semantics, and the
  serialisation round-trip that this run caught), plus decision-side resolution
  guards in tests/test_decision.py.

gate: 1168 -> 1191 (+23)
